CODE-REVIEW ENGINE (high) — R5 round 1 — range 75b85ac8..a0d3585c

The `ReportFindings` tool is not available in this session (ToolSearch returned no match), so the findings are stated here directly. Scope: `git diff develop...HEAD` on the worktree (five commits, wave 1 of the plan; the docs/site/own-config drift is U6/U7's and out of scope).

**Findings**

1. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/SKILL.md:253` — HIGH — init's own clone row is only the bare command (`git submodule update --init <path>`) and the file says init is "named in neither list" of `membership.md`, so the new branch-checkout step at `membership.md:129` never runs inside init. Under submodule linkage a member init clones in this run arrives detached, and the new detached-member rule (`existing-repo.md:1128`) then refuses it in the same run. `existing-repo.md:1136` and `new-repo.md` §11 both claim "a member this run cloned never arrives detached" — false for init's own clone path.

2. `plugins/vwf/assets/membership.md:129` — HIGH — after `git submodule update --init`, checking out the branch `origin/HEAD` names (usually `main`) moves the member's working tree to that branch's tip, not the commit the base's gitlink records (typically a `develop` commit). Concrete result: `plan`/`execute`/`doctor` survey the wrong code, the base's `git status` shows the gitlink as modified, and the base's `code:merge:*` refuses on `has_uncommitted_changes`. The checkout should be a branch at the recorded commit (`git checkout -B <branch>` at HEAD) or the branch containing it.

3. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/references/new-repo.md:789` vs `existing-repo.md:1243` — MEDIUM — the two references disagree on what "mainline" is. new-repo says "the branch the repo stood on at the HEAD read"; existing-repo says "read from `origin/HEAD` or from the branch the repo is on". A repo with neither `main` nor `develop` whose user invoked the run on `feat/x` (origin/HEAD = `master`) gets `main` created from the feature branch under new-repo's rule, `develop` from that, the ops commit on it, and both pushed — unmerged feature work published as `main`.

4. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/references/new-repo.md:654` — MEDIUM — the (a) table preselects the pack defaults (`direct`/`pr`) "the same for every repo", ignoring the value an existing file carries. A repo shaped under the old pack with an untouched `MERGE_MODEL = "direct"` file has a matching lock hash, so a reshape replaces the file outright and asks (a) with `pr` preselected for `main`; accepting the defaults silently flips `main` to `pr` and the forge pass then adds a require-PR rule the user never chose. The legacy value (or the current pair) should seed the preselection.

5. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/references/existing-repo.md:1248` — MEDIUM — the checkout refusal is attributed to one cause only (a run-written file differing between branches). `git checkout develop` also refuses when `develop` is checked out in another worktree — this repo's own layout (`.worktrees/<plan>` with `develop` in the main checkout) — and in a linked worktree the switch is impossible altogether. The run then reports a wrong unlock ("the checkout") that cannot be performed there.

6. `plugins/vwf/skills/doctor/references/stack-checks.md:504` — LOW/MEDIUM — the drift row's remedy "reshape writes the pair" is unreachable for a kept file: `existing-repo.md:533` says a `kept_files` entry is never re-offered, and new-repo (a) writes the pair only where the file is landed or replaced. A user who customised the `[env]` block (a hash mismatch surviving the splice) and kept it gets the legacy row on every doctor run with no remedy that clears it, short of hand-editing `.config/vwf.yaml` — which nothing tells them.

7. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/references/new-repo.md:731` vs `existing-repo.md` ("Before staging anything, do this repo's branch work") — LOW — ordering contradiction: new-repo stages at (b) then checks out `develop` at (c); existing-repo checks out before staging. Same git outcome in most cases, but a skill reader following (b)→(c) on a repo with commits does the checkout with a populated index, which is the case git refuses more often.

8. `plugins/vwf/skills/init/references/new-repo.md:382` — LOW — §7 now calls the landing pair "the ninth and tenth" positions while the same section numbers them "fourth, fifth and sixth" (line ~432) and gives ninth/tenth to the runtime positions (line ~487). An implementer counting the splice set from this paragraph gets the wrong pair.

No issues found in the shell changes: `_scripts/merge`'s per-destination `case` and legacy fallback are `set -u`-safe and only ever reached with `develop`/`main`; the git-workflow `read_env` snippet matches `mise env -s bash`'s `export KEY=value` form and `^export MERGE_MODEL=` cannot match the `_DEVELOP`/`_MAIN` keys.
